--- body ---

## Context

Exp1 (parametric, no web) measures what frontier models recall about the
Vietnam thermal fleet from memory. We currently report the F1 ceiling but
have **no explicit upper bound the reader can judge it against**. The author
deliberately published a derivative of the reference (the MOIT/PDP8 plant
list) on Wikipedia *before* the experiment — which is why Protocol §3.4 bans
Wikipedia as a **justification/citation** source.

The key distinction that makes this a fair bar: the protocol bans Wikipedia
**as justification**, but **allows any source for lead generation** (and
encourages uncertainty expression). A model is therefore fully permitted to
*recall* a Wikipedia-seeded plant — it just may not *cite* Wikipedia for it.
So Wikipedia coverage of the reference is the **recall bar a competent
parametric model ought to clear**: the answer key was placed directly into the
training corpus. Scoring below it is not "missing obscure facts" — it is
failing to retrieve knowledge the models demonstrably have.

The bar has **two regimes** (measured 2026-06-09, coal subset, against the
180-plant v2.3 reference; see `data/reference/raw/wikipedia_coal_vietnam-2026-06-09.wikitext`):

- **Built fleet** (operating 92%, construction/permitted 100%): high bar.
  Models recalling fewer than ~92% of operating plants underperform an
  available ceiling — this is the quantified "disappointing."
- **Forward-looking pipeline** (proposed/announced ≈ 33%): low bar. Even
  Wikipedia barely carries these, so models *structurally cannot* recall them.
  This is not model failure — it is the **reference's unique contribution**
  (the PDP8 pipeline no parametric source holds).

Showing the bar early (not just asserting "disappointing" in the conclusion)
lets the reader carry it through §4–§7.

## Relationship to other tickets

- **0486** (GEM coverage) is the *independent reproduction* concordance — GEM
  is a genuinely external tracker. THIS ticket is the *parametric recall bar* —
  Wikipedia is the author's own seeded derivative. Different purpose, different
  source epistemics; do not merge the two into one "coverage by source" table
  (it would conflate reproduction with contamination).
- Depends on resolving the **173-vs-180** manuscript/data count first (the
  reference is canonically 180 = v2.3; the manuscript still says 173).

## Relevant files

- `slides/manuscript/main.md` — §2 quality bar / §4 setup (where the bar is introduced); §7 conclusion (where it is revisited)
- `data/reference/raw/wikipedia_coal_vietnam-2026-06-09.wikitext` — cached coal list (raw, reproducible)
- `data/reference/raw/wikipedia_power_vietnam-2026-06-09.wikitext` — cached general list (has the gas/CCGT plants needed to complete the thermal bar)
- `src/aedist/reconcile.py`, `src/aedist/evaluate.py` (`load_plants_csv`, `reference_plant_count`) — matcher + reference loader
- prototype: `experiments/exploration/0486/explore_bars.py` (committed; already coal+gas — parses the general page `==Gas turbines==` section)

## Actions

1. Complete the bar: parse the gas/CCGT plants from the general power-stations
   wikitext too, so the bar covers all 180 reference plants, not just the 88 coal.
2. Build a reproducible script (cached wikitext in → coverage-by-status table out),
   wired in the Makefile (one output per rule). HITL-triage residual non-matches
   (real absence vs matcher naming-variant), reusing the recognised/unrecognised
   machinery — do not report raw matcher coverage as "Wikipedia coverage."
3. Introduce the bar early in `main.md` and add one sentence in §7 closing the loop.
4. Frame explicitly with the justification-vs-lead-generation distinction so a
   reviewer cannot call it circular.

## Test

```python
def test_wikipedia_bar_table_has_two_regimes():
    df = pd.read_csv("data/reference/tab_wikipedia_recall_bar.csv")
    built = df[df.status.isin(["operating", "construction", "permitted"])].coverage.mean()
    pipeline = df[df.status.isin(["proposed", "announced"])].coverage.mean()
    assert built > 0.85 and pipeline < 0.6  # the two-regime claim must hold on real data
```

## Verification

- [ ] Bar covers all 180 reference plants (coal + gas), not coal-only
- [ ] Residual non-matches HITL-triaged (gap vs matcher miss)
- [ ] Bar introduced early in `main.md` and revisited in §7
- [ ] Justification-vs-lead-generation framing stated where the bar is introduced
- [ ] Raw wikitext snapshots committed under `data/reference/raw/` with provenance
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- Do not weaken Protocol §3.4 (Wikipedia banned as justification) — this ticket uses it, does not relax it
- Denominator = `reference_plant_count()` (currently 180), never a hardcoded literal

## Exit criteria

- The Wikipedia recall bar is a generated artifact, shown early, framed correctly, and the §4 underperformance reads as "below an available ceiling"
